SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: the corners at vertices where three or more stripes end are
computed before those of two or one (after a tangent ride's corners, as
before), so that the result does not depend on the order the contours
were given in (mitcad#1987).

The reported design's Fillet1 (GitHub #371): a pit's wall foot rounded
with 1 on one side and 0.8 on the other, 2.5 long, between a corner of
three at one end (with the plate's rounding of 5 along the pit's edge
and the pit's 45 degree face's rounding of 1) and a corner of two at the
other (with the fin arm's end). Corners are computed in the order their
vertices came in, which follows the contours' order. With the contours
as the test lists them the corner of three came first and the result was
tight; with the top's 0.5 given right after the foot's 3, as MitCad's
fillet gives the file's sets, the corner of two came first and the
corner of three then left vertices 0.54 and 0.66 off their edges (and
0.14 mm3 more material). Patch 1730 made Chamfer3 build on that result;
the vertices are now tight in either order and the two orders give the
same body.

- Compute, the corners' order (anOrder): three passes, the tangent rides'
  corners of two stripes first (as before), then the corners of three or
  more stripes, then the rest.

Test: geometry/tests/test_fillet_top_chains.cpp, test_whole_fillet1: the
whole of Fillet1 on the case `fillet-wall-run-on` of
MITCAD_GEOMETRY_CASES in the listed order and with the top's 0.5 right
after the foot's 3: every vertex within 0.05 and the same volume (without
the patch 0.66 and 0.14 mm3 apart).

Upstream: new.

